Menhir was one of [[Aut]]'s siblings, called the Heroes: the representation of a heroic party, and the counterpart of [[Divinity/The Narrative/Insuperlex|Insuperlex]] the Mountain. It appeared as a ring of standing stones, no two alike, that could not be counted or kept track of once one looked away, and it spoke in harmonies rather than words. Its notes were not the [[Phenomena/Harmony/Voidsong|Voidsong]]; those who heard both understood that the Menhir was speaking a language of its own, the way a god speaks to a god.

It showed those who came to it what could be rather than what would be, and it was old beyond reckoning. In answer to the Party's plea it gave them [[Phenomena/The End|The End]], a firearm of white ivory and strange gold, with a plain card box holding seventeen gold spikes to fit its chamber.

Menhir is dead. One of those spikes, fired into a single stone, brought down the whole ring, and the rest of the narrative felt it die at once. It was the first of the five to die, and four remain.

Long before, on [[Geography/Ushas/index|Ushas]], the party that would found [[Org/Sedecium|Sedecium]] came on a patch of grass floating in [[Phenomena/The Pale|the Pale]] with standing stones on it, one of them toppled. They set it upright, and were blessed with a boon that showed them the path forward, to a second way into the [[Geography/Ushas/Undermountain|Undermountain]]. They never knew it as the Menhir, nor that the Narrative existed; to them it was a pile of rocks.
